<div class="tab {{tabs.battlefields.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="battlefields">

{{! Its own tab rather than a panel inside Combat. A Battlefield is a thing the whole
    table is standing in rather than a number on one character, and the Combat tab is
    already a column of this character's own tracking values.

    Every rule here is about this character alone. What the rest of the map is like is
    the table's - nothing in this system has Squares to ask - so what is on this tab is
    set by the player, or by whatever else the table decides. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">
      Light Level
      <span class="panel-hint">Set by you, or by an effect</span>
    </h3>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="What the light where you are standing is doing to you. Too bright and too dark cost the same: the table is symmetrical about Normal.">
        <em>Level</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.lightLevel" data-dtype="Number">
          {{#each lightLevels as |level|}}
          <option value="{{level.value}}" {{#if level.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{level.label}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    {{#if lightLevel.effect}}
    {{! What the chosen Level is doing, in the rulebook's own words. Drawn only when it is
        doing something: Normal says "no effect", and a row repeating that is a row to
        read past. }}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{lightLevel.effect}}</p>
    {{/if}}
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">
      Cover
      <span class="panel-hint">Turned on by you, for as long as it lasts</span>
    </h3>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="A Feature between you and whoever is attacking you. Nothing here knows where anything is standing, so this is yours to turn on and off.">
        <em>Behind cover</em>
        <input type="checkbox" name="system.battlefield.cover.active"
               {{#if system.battlefield.cover.active}}checked{{/if}}/>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="What the Feature you are behind is made of. Twice its Hardness Value comes off the Damage you would suffer, and the Value is worked out from your base Tier of Power.">
        <em>Hardness</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.cover.rank" data-dtype="Number">
          {{#each hardnessRanks as |hardness|}}
          <option value="{{hardness.rank}}" {{#if hardness.behind}}selected{{/if}}>Rank {{hardness.rank}} &middot; {{hardness.material}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    {{#if cover.note}}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{cover.note}}</p>
    {{/if}}

    {{! The one case the rule takes itself back in. Said beside the toggle, because that is
        where somebody deciding whether to leave it on is looking. }}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">
      Cover does not apply if the Attacking Maneuver has an Area of Effect that includes
      the Feature as a target. Areas are the table's here: turn this off for that attack.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">
      Battle Terrain
      <span class="panel-hint">Features are the table's to keep</span>
    </h3>

    {{#if featureAttacks.length}}
    <div class="maneuver-list">
      {{#each featureAttacks as |attack|}}
      <button type="button" class="dbu-clash-button" data-action="attackFeature"
              data-item-id="{{attack.itemId}}"
              data-tooltip="Throw it at a Feature rather than at a Character. No Strike Roll and no Wound Roll: the card says the Damage.">
        {{attack.name}}
      </button>
      {{/each}}
    </div>
    {{else}}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">
      No Attacking Maneuvers to throw at a Feature yet.
    </p>
    {{/if}}

    {{! The other direction. Most collisions in these rules are not a card this system
        opened - walked into, fallen onto, pushed by something that never rolled - and
        this is where a player says one happened. }}
    <div class="maneuver-list">
      <button type="button" class="dbu-clash-button" data-action="takeCollision"
              data-tooltip="You hit something. Pick what it was made of and the damage is worked out from your base Tier of Power.">
        Receive Collision Damage
      </button>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{! Hardness, for reading. Two numbers wearing one name, and the one that matters at the
      table is not stored anywhere - the Value is worked out against whoever is taking the
      damage, so the same wall costs two characters two different numbers. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <details class="condition-group" data-section="hardness"
             {{#if openSections.hardness}}open{{/if}}>
      <summary class="panel-title">
        Hardness
        <span class="panel-note">Your Values, at base Tier {{baseTierOfPower}}</span>
      </summary>

      {{! Said first, and said in the rules' own words. The Value is not something the
          Collision Damage is worked out from - it is the Collision Damage. }}
      <p class="tab-placeholder">
        <strong>{{collisionDamage}}</strong>
      </p>

      <p class="tab-placeholder">
        The Hardness Rank is what a Feature is made of, and is also its Life Points. The
        Hardness Value is what hitting it costs - twice the Rank a base Tier of Power,
        except Rank 0, which is 1(bT). The Values below are yours: they scale with the
        base Tier of Power of whoever suffers the Collision Damage, so another character
        hitting the same wall takes a different number.
      </p>

      <ul class="quality-list">
        {{#each hardnessRanks as |hardness|}}
        <li class="quality">
          <strong>Rank {{hardness.rank}} <span class="quality-mark">{{hardness.value}} Damage to you</span></strong>
          <span>{{hardness.text}}</span>
        </li>
        {{/each}}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>

  {{! Battle Weather, for reading. The frame ahead of the Weathers themselves: the three
      Tiers, and the rules that hold whichever one is in play.

      Nothing to set here yet. A Battle Weather stands over some number of Squares and this
      system has none, so when the Weathers arrive it will take the shape Light Level
      already has - the player says which one they are standing in and at what Tier. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <details class="condition-group" data-section="weather"
             {{#if openSections.weather}}open{{/if}}>
      <summary class="panel-title">
        Battle Weather
        <span class="panel-note">The Weathers themselves are not here yet</span>
      </summary>

      <ul class="quality-list">
        {{#each weatherTiers as |tier|}}
        <li class="quality">
          <strong>{{tier.name}} <span class="quality-mark">Tier {{tier.tier}}</span></strong>
          <span>{{tier.text}}</span>
        </li>
        {{/each}}
      </ul>

      <ul class="quality-list">
        {{#each weatherRules as |rule|}}
        <li class="quality">
          <strong>{{rule.name}}</strong>
          <span>{{rule.text}}</span>
        </li>
        {{/each}}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>

  {{! Feature Qualities, for reading. A Quality belongs to a Feature and a Feature is the
      ARC's, so there is nothing here to set - this is the list, in the rules' own words,
      kept where somebody deciding what a wall is made of can find it.

      Six of them are asked about when Collision Damage is applied, which is the one
      moment any of this reaches a character. The list is the same list. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <details class="condition-group" data-section="featureQualities"
             {{#if openSections.featureQualities}}open{{/if}}>
      <summary class="panel-title">
        Feature Qualities
        <span class="panel-note">The ARC's, for reading</span>
      </summary>

      <p class="tab-placeholder">
        The material is what a Square, Feature or other object is made out of. Generally
        the ARC decides the material and picks an appropriate Hardness Rank for it - and
        some Features have Qualities on top of that, decided the same way.
      </p>

      <ul class="quality-list">
        {{#each featureQualities as |quality|}}
        <li class="quality">
          <strong>{{quality.name}}{{#if quality.onCollision}} <span
            class="quality-mark" data-tooltip="Offered when Collision Damage is applied, because that is the moment it does something.">asked on collision</span>{{/if}}</strong>
          <span>{{quality.text}}</span>
          {{#if quality.note}}<em>{{quality.note}}</em>{{/if}}
        </li>
        {{/each}}
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>
</div>
